  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nAn upper level lower pressure system currently spinning over\nIdaho, will move into Wyoming this evening, and eventually into\nwestern South Dakota by Sunday evening. This will bring abundant\ncloud cover to western and southern portions of the forecast \narea through the weekend. In addition, areas mainly south and \nwest of Lake Sakakawea and the Missouri River will see  \naccumulating precipitation.\n\nCurrently, low stratus and fog covered much of southwest and \nsouth central ND. Portions of the northwest and north central \nwere free of low stratus, but remained covered with mid and high\nlevel clouds. For the most part, western and central ND \nremained precipitation free early this Saturday morning.\nHowever, patchy light drizzle remained over portions of the \narea. At 3 AM CDT, very light drizzle was falling at WFO \nBismarck. There were a couple of webcams along I-94 depicting \nsome light drizzle as well. Drizzle was more widespread around \nmidnight in this area, but there is some still falling. Luckily \nthe impacts are negligible. There are some lower vsbys, mainly \n1-3SM in the south, but with widespread stratus south and high \ncloud cover north, the threat for widespread dense fog looks to \nbe low.\n\nFor today, will maintain the forecast temperatures introduced\nlast evening with slightly cooler high temperatures than\nguidance, given the widespread low cloud cover. Will keep a\nmention of drizzle in the south central through 12Z and will\nre-evaluate. Otherwise just a slight chance of rain along the \nslow moving cold fron dropping south from Canada, and slight \nchance, increasing to chance pops in the far west central and \nsouthwest, and slowly advancing eastward.\n\nTonight, and through the day Sunday will bring categorical rain\nover the far southwest, with medium to high chances for rain \nalong the Missouri River. HREF probabilities of 24 hour \nprecipitation of at least a half inch, from 7 PM Saturday \nthrough 7 PM Sunday, range from 40 to 80 percent, right along \nthe Montana and South Dakota borders, from northern Golden \nValley County south to Bowman County and from Bowman county east\nthrough Adams County. The probability for at least a tenth of \nan inch for this same timeframe, ranges from less than 10 \npercent just north and east of the Missouri River to greater \nthan 40 percent only a few miles south and west of the Missouri.\n\nPrecipitation is expected to taper from north to south, west of\nthe Missouri River Sunday night, with dry conditions expected \nby Monday morning. It looks like a couple of dry days to begin \nthe work week, Monday and Tuesday. We then see a couple of low \nwaves tracking through the area, the first around mid-week and \nthe next over the weekend. NBM is currently depicting low to \nmoderate precipitation chances (20 to 40%) with each wave. After\nsome cooler temperatures this weekend, the NBM is advertising a\nwarm-up through the work week. Ensemble spreads, at least \nthrough around Thursday, are noticeably tighter than the past \ncouple of day, thus confidence is increasing that we see a \ngradual warm-up through the week. On Thursday current NBM \ntemperatures range from the lower 70s east to the lower 80s \nwest.",
